<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> I'm Happy.</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Are You?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading_1: Match spoken words to their written form as they are heard and supported with pictures.
reading_2: Participate in choral reading sessions, supported by the teacher.
phonemic_awareness: Recognize familiar letters.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can match pictures of facial expressions (e.g., smiling, frowning) to words like 'happy' or 'sad'.
Can recognize simple emotion-related words in choral reading with support from the teacher (e.g., clapping when hearing the word 'happy' and pointing to a picture of a sad face when hearing the word 'sad' during choral reading).
Can recognize letters of the alphabet in familiar words related to feelings and emotions (e.g., pointing to the letter 'H' in the word 'happy' and saying 'H').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage activates prior knowledge related to feelings and emotion words introduced in Lesson 1, preparing students for reading.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying large flashcards of facial expressions (happy, sad, angry, tired, excited) around the classroom. The teacher asks, "How are you today, friends?" and models different feelings with facial expressions and body language. Students point to the corresponding flashcard as the teacher says the feeling, e.g., "I'm happy!" while pointing to the 'happy' flashcard. Students repeat chorally.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture of a smiling child and says, "Look! She is happy. I'm happy!" The teacher traces the word 'happy' on a large card while saying it, emphasizing the /h/ sound. The teacher then shows a picture of a child frowning and says, "Oh, he is sad. I'm sad," and traces the word 'sad'. This visually connects the spoken word to its written form. The teacher repeats with 'excited', highlighting the /e/ sound and letter 'E'.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a happy face picture) "Is she sad or happy?" (Expected: "Happy!")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing the word 'sad' with a picture of a sad face) "What is this feeling?" (Expected: "Sad!")</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "I'm Happy.")</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are exposed to emotion-related words in written form, supported by visuals, within a simple text.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, simple picture book or a big chart paper story titled "How Are You, Friends?" with 3-4 short sentences per page. Each sentence features one feeling (e.g., "I'm happy!", "I'm sad.", "I'm tired.") accompanied by a large, clear picture of a child expressing that emotion. The target words ('happy', 'sad', 'tired', 'excited') are highlighted or written in a distinct color.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student can understand and respond to simple written information about feelings and emotions when supported by pictures.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the "How Are You, Friends?" story aloud, pointing to each word as they read, and emphasizing the target feeling words.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> The teacher asks students to point to the picture that shows "happy" when they hear the word 'happy' in the story. Similarly, "Point to 'sad'," "Point to 'tired'."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Word-Picture Matching:</b> The teacher reads a sentence (e.g., "I'm happy.") and asks students to find the written word 'happy' on the page and then point to the corresponding picture. This is done for 'happy', 'sad', 'tired', and 'excited'.</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students practice recognizing the written form of emotion words and identifying familiar letters within them, directly addressing the reading objectives.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading: Matching words to pictures, choral reading, recognizing letters of the alphabet in familiar words. Grammar: 'I'm + adjective'. Vocabulary: happy, sad, tired, excited.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Word-Picture Bingo:</b> Each student receives a "Feelings Bingo" card with 6-9 pictures of different facial expressions (happy, sad, tired, hungry, thirsty, sleepy, excited, sick, angry). The teacher holds up large word cards (e.g., 'happy', 'sad', 'tired'). When the teacher shows a word card and says the word, students find the matching picture on their bingo card and cover it with a counter. The first student to get three in a row calls "Bingo!"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Choral Story Reading & Letter Hunt:</b> The teacher displays the "How Are You, Friends?" story again. Students participate in choral reading, repeating sentences after the teacher while the teacher points to the words. The teacher then asks students to find specific letters in familiar words, e.g., "Point to the letter 'H' in 'happy'!" "Point to the letter 'E' in 'excited'!" "Can you find the letter 'S' in 'sad'?" The teacher can also introduce new letters like 'W' from 'warm' and 'Y' from 'you' in 'How are you?'.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Feeling Word Matching (Individual Work):</b> Students are given a worksheet with 4-5 pictures of feelings and corresponding word boxes (e.g., 'happy', 'sad', 'tired', 'excited'). Below each picture, there are three word options. Students draw a line from the picture to the correct feeling word. The teacher circulates, providing support and checking for understanding.</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "I'm Happy.")</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students actively engage in reading recognition within a group setting, applying their learned vocabulary and letter recognition.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Choral Reading and Word Recognition Activity.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher divides students into small groups. Each group receives a set of large flashcards, each containing a simple sentence (e.g., "I'm happy.", "I'm sad.", "I'm tired.") with a corresponding picture. The teacher calls out a feeling (e.g., "Show me 'I'm excited'!") and each group must quickly find the correct flashcard, hold it up, and chorally "read" the sentence by pointing to the words. The teacher ensures that students are pointing to the correct words and can identify familiar letters within the words. For example, when they hold up "I'm happy", the teacher might ask one student, "Can you show me the 'H' in 'happy'?" The activity continues with various feeling words, encouraging quick recognition and simple "reading."</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage assesses students' ability to match spoken words to written words and recognize letters within the context of feelings.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the Performance/Production stage and a quick exit ticket activity.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete a "Match the Feeling" exit ticket. Each ticket has three pictures of different facial expressions (e.g., happy, sad, excited). Below each picture are three written words, only one of which matches the picture. Students circle the correct word. Additionally, the teacher asks individual students to point to a specific letter (e.g., 'H', 'E', 'S') in one of the words they circled.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Matches spoken words to their written form with picture support (e.g., circles 'happy' for a smiling face).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes familiar letters (e.g., points to 'H' in 'happy').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participates in choral reading sessions by pointing to words.</li>
            </ul></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I find the word 'happy' when I see a happy face?" (Students give a thumbs up/down).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I find the letter 'H' in 'happy'?" (Students show with fingers: 1-yes, 2-a little, 3-no).</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage encourages both student and teacher to reflect on the learning experience and plan for future lessons.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new feeling words can you read today?" (e.g., happy, sad).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite activity to help you read the feeling words?"</li>
            </ul></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading lesson? (e.g., visual supports, choral reading, letter recognition).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to support students' reading comprehension, particularly for those struggling with letter recognition? (e.g., more one-on-one letter tracing, smaller group phonics games).</li>
            </ul></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction) will build on the recognized words by having students actively use 'I'm + adjective' statements to express their feelings in simple conversations and role-plays. The vocabulary and foundational reading recognition established in this lesson will enable more confident oral production.</li></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>